-- allium: 3
-- sync.allium
--
-- Scope: This board's relationship with the shared store — coming up, staying
--        up, coming back, and what it says while it is down. Also what a board
--        asks the store to send it, the far more important question of what it
--        does not, where the rows it draws come from, and where the changes it
--        makes go.
-- Includes: the connection's lifecycle and the retry that drives it; the
--           identity handshake that must complete before anything syncs;
--           subscribing to and unsubscribing from an epic; the rule that stops
--           a board whose identity no longer matches; what an operator sees
--           while the store is unreachable; WHERE THE BOARD READS ITS ROWS
--           FROM, and what it draws when the answer is unreachable; WHERE A
--           CHANGE GOES, what happens to one made while the store is down, and
--           what the store does with one it will not accept.
-- Excludes: which store, and how it is spoken to — nothing here names a
--           protocol, a socket or a vendor, and a correct implementation over
--           any transport satisfies this file. The shape of what travels (see
--           core.allium: Task, Epic, Host, Subscription). The snapshot escape
--           hatch and the id-sequence obligation (see spacetime-seed.allium).
--           Whose identity this install holds and how it got it (see
--           host.allium: AdoptUserIdentity and the two rules beside it) — this
--           file offers the identity and consumes the verdict, and deliberately
--           does not decide it. WHICH rows a card is DRAWN from once they are
--           here (see board-layout.allium, board-visuals.allium) — this file
--           says where they came from and says nothing about their arrangement.
--           WHICH mutations exist and what each one means (see tasks.allium,
--           epics.allium) — this file says only where a mutation
--           GOES and what happens when it cannot get there. Anything that
--           spawns a process: dispatching an agent, provisioning or releasing
--           a worktree, running a feed's command. Those are jobs for the
--           machine the operator is sitting at and no store can do them, so
--           they stay local whatever this file says about rows.
--
-- Why this exists as its own file. host.allium answers "who am I?" and
-- core.allium answers "what is a task?"; neither is the right home for a thing
-- that is up or down. A connection has a lifecycle, a failure mode that repeats,
-- and a visible state — which is to say it is a domain entity, and it did not
-- have one before.
--
-- THE HONEST SHAPE OF THE PROBLEM. A shared store reachable over a network is
-- down sometimes. Not as an exception: as a matter of course, for a few seconds,
-- several times a day, whenever a laptop lid closes or a VPN reconnects. A
-- design that treats disconnection as an error to report once has specified the
-- rare case and left the common one to whatever the code happens to do. So the
-- states below are not "working" and "broken". They are the four states a
-- connection is actually ever in, and three of them are ordinary.
--
-- ONE OF THEM IS NOT ORDINARY, and it is the only fatal thing in this file: the
-- store answering with a different person than the one this install has stored.
-- See StopOnAUserIdentityConflict, and host.allium: RefuseAChangedUserIdentity
-- for why that is worth stopping a board over when nothing else here is.
--
-- EVERY BOARD HAS A STORE. A launch that names none runs dispatch's own
-- managed store on loopback, starting it and publishing its module first where
-- needed (startup.allium: BringUpTheManagedStoreOnceTheHostIsNamed). By the
-- time OpenBoardConnection fires, configured_store_server() therefore always
-- names a store: the one the operator gave, or the managed one. Everything
-- below holds for every board.
--
-- AND NO BOARD HAS A LOCAL DATABASE. No local database is opened by the
-- board, by MCP or by any subcommand (storage.allium: NoSqlite), and nothing
-- is migrated out of a leftover copy (storage.allium:
-- CodeNeverTouchesLegacyDatabase).
--
-- A STORE THAT IS DOWN MEANS A BOARD WITH NO CARDS ON IT. A board reads only
-- the store. There is no fallback to a local copy and there must not be one:
-- core.allium's shared tables have exactly one copy, and a board that
-- silently served an older one would be showing work whose current state it
-- cannot see — a colleague's task that has since been finished, reassigned or
-- deleted, drawn as though it were live. An empty board with an outage on it is
-- a worse experience and a truer one. See AConnectedBoardDrawsOnlyDeliveredRows
-- and ADisconnectedBoardDrawsNoSharedRows below.
--
-- AND IT CANNOT WRITE EITHER. The same bargain, on the other side: a board
-- whose store is down refuses the mutation, says so, and changes nothing
-- anywhere. It does not queue it for later and it does not fall back to a
-- local copy. A queue would be a second authority over a shared row — replayed
-- minutes later, on top of whatever a colleague did meanwhile, with the
-- operator's original intent long since acted on elsewhere. See
-- BoardWritesThroughTheStore and NoWriteIsEverQueued.

use "./core.allium" as core
use "./startup.allium" as startup
use "./host.allium" as host
use "./spacetime-seed.allium" as seed

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities and Variants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- This board's one connection to the shared store.
--
-- Every drawn board has exactly one. It is created, and its first attempt
-- answered, before the board draws (OpenBoardConnection); a board that could
-- not get that far did not start (startup.allium:
-- AbortWhenTheStoreCannotBeReached).
entity BoardConnection {
    -- Where the shared store is. A field rather than config because it is per
    -- install, not per product: two people share a store by both naming it, and
    -- a default would silently point a board at somewhere nobody chose.
    server: String

    status: connecting | connected | disconnected | failed

    -- Consecutive failed attempts since the last success. Reset on connect,
    -- not decayed: this counts the current outage, and an outage that ends is
    -- over rather than partly over. Its only consumer is the backoff.
    attempts: Integer

    -- What went wrong, in terms an operator can act on. Present exactly while
    -- something is wrong, which is what makes the pair of states below
    -- self-describing rather than needing a reader to remember which one
    -- carries a reason.
    last_error: String when status = disconnected | failed

    -- When the outage began, in the sense the backoff cares about: the moment
    -- of the most recent failure, not of the first. Present only while
    -- retrying, and cleared on the way out, so it cannot be read as the age of
    -- an outage that has ended.
    last_failure_at: Timestamp when status = disconnected

    -- When RetryAfterBackoff below fires. Exponential in the number of
    -- consecutive failures, from config.reconnect_backoff_base, and never
    -- beyond config.reconnect_backoff_max past the last failure.
    --
    -- A derived instant rather than a stored delay, because a delay has to be
    -- counted down by something and an instant is simply true or not yet. It
    -- also makes the retry a temporal trigger like every other scheduled thing
    -- in this system, rather than a timer the board has to remember to keep.
    next_attempt_at: last_failure_at + min(
        config.reconnect_backoff_base * pow(2, attempts - 1),
        config.reconnect_backoff_max
    )

    -- The epics this board has asked for: those the identity's own
    -- core/Subscription rows name (the own_subscriptions view). Derived from
    -- the shared rows rather than held here, so that subscribing on the
    -- desktop is already true on the laptop — the subscription belongs to the
    -- PERSON, and the connection is a machine's.
    subscribed_epics: followed_epics_of(core/local_host().owner)

    -- The epics whose rows this board asks for: every followed epic and every
    -- sub-epic beneath one, AT ANY DEPTH. Following an epic is following its
    -- tree — the board draws an epic's card from its whole subtree
    -- (epics.allium), so a follow that stopped at the first level would put a
    -- parent card on a teammate's board with the tree underneath it missing.
    -- Real boards nest four levels deep; no fixed depth is the right one.
    --
    -- Derived, like subscribed_epics, and for the same reason: the Subscription
    -- rows say what the person chose, and the tree says what that choice
    -- reaches. Nobody follows a sub-epic by hand to see it. The store
    -- derives it (the covered_epics view), so it follows a follow live.
    covered_epics: covered_epics_of(core/local_host().owner)

    -- WHAT THIS MEANS FOR A FEED'S POLL OWNER. The feed runner picks the
    -- feed epics it polls from the epics this board holds: covered_epics
    -- together with the own_epics view (an epic this identity created is held
    -- whether or not anyone follows it). A feed epic this host neither follows
    -- nor created is never polled here. Everything after the poll runs in
    -- the module's ingest_feed, over every row the store holds rather than
    -- over this connection's rows: the retired-item check
    -- (spacetime/module/src/feed_ingest.rs::retired_without_task), the
    -- routing into sub-epics and the stale-subtree delete. So a poll owner
    -- that holds only part of a feed epic's subtree never reads a survivor
    -- as absent.
    --
    -- A CLAIM DOES NOT OUTLIVE THE FOLLOW. UnsubscribeFromEpic releases the
    -- poll claims of this person's hosts on every feed epic the unfollow
    -- stops covering, so the feed pauses until another host that holds the
    -- epic claims it; it never silently stops for every board.

    -- WHAT A CONNECTION OWNS BESIDES ITS STATUS: the rows its subscriptions
    -- have delivered.
    --
    -- Not a field, because the rows are of every shared entity at once and this
    -- language has no union to type that with. It is stated here rather than
    -- left implicit because the OWNERSHIP is the load-bearing part:
    -- ADisconnectedBoardDrawsNoSharedRows is a consequence of the rows
    -- belonging to the connection, not an extra rule somebody has to remember.
    --
    -- A set that outlived its connection would be a cache, and a cache is
    -- precisely what must not exist here: core.allium's shared tables have one
    -- copy, and a second one served during an outage would draw a colleague's
    -- finished work as though it were live. See
    -- AConnectedBoardDrawsOnlyDeliveredRows below.
    --
    -- The implementation's counterpart is `SharedRows` (`src/sync/rows.rs`),
    -- held beside the transport. Where it sits does not matter; that it is
    -- emptied with the connection does.

    @invariant AConnectedBoardDrawsOnlyDeliveredRows
        -- On a board with a connection, every shared row on screen arrived
        -- through that connection's subscriptions. There is no second reader.
        --
        -- ONE SOURCE. Every drawn board has a connection (OpenBoardConnection
        -- runs before it draws), so there is no second branch. Not "the
        -- subscription, falling back to local": a shared table has exactly
        -- one copy, so any other copy is not a cache of the store but
        -- whatever some install last wrote, which may be months stale and may
        -- name tasks that no longer exist. Serving it during an outage would
        -- put a colleague's finished work back on screen as though it were
        -- live.
        --
        -- EVERY SHARED READ, NOT ONLY THE DRAWING. "Drawn" here covers every
        -- read of a shared row the board's own process makes — an MCP tool
        -- looking up a task, a setting read back after it was saved, the
        -- subscribed-epic list, the feed runner's epic list — and not only
        -- the reads that put cards on screen. Every `store::Store` read
        -- answers from the connection's `SharedRows`, the rows its writes'
        -- reducers land in (`store_connection::StoreParts::build`), so a task
        -- created a moment ago is found.
        --
        -- A ROW THAT CANNOT BE UNDERSTOOD IS DROPPED, not defaulted and not
        -- fatal. A task carrying a status this binary does not know is
        -- missing from the board rather than sitting on it under a plausible
        -- wrong one, and the rows beside it are unaffected. A row whose
        -- status was REMOVED (config.removed_statuses) is dropped too, but
        -- quietly and by a different rule: see
        -- RowsWithARemovedStatusAreDropped.
        --
        -- The counterpart to SubscriptionsCoverOnlyTheOwnBoardAndItsEpics,
        -- which constrains the ASK. This constrains the READ, and the pair is
        -- what makes the containment claim complete: asking only for your own
        -- rows protects nobody if the reader can also reach around the
        -- subscription to everyone else's.
        --
        -- Not stated as an expression because the two sides — what was
        -- delivered and what is drawn — are sets of every shared entity at
        -- once, which this language has no union to name. What is checkable is
        -- a property of the CODE rather than of a state: exactly one read path
        -- exists (`crate::store::BoardReads`, whose card reads are the store's
        -- own read traits), and the `Store` that implements it holds no handle
        -- to any other store to reach through. `store::tests::over_sync::board_reads`
        -- asserts it directly, by standing a fully populated store beside one
        -- whose rows are empty and requiring every read of the second to
        -- answer empty.
        --
        -- There is no install without a connection to except from this: the
        -- store is mandatory (see this file's header).

    -- Whether startup has finished with this connection. False from creation
    -- until the first attempt has been answered and its subscription applied;
    -- true for the rest of the board's life. It is what tells the FIRST
    -- attempt — synchronous, and fatal if it fails (OpenBoardConnection) —
    -- from every later one, which is an ordinary outage.
    board_drawn: Boolean

    -- A connection of this run has settled the identity and applied its
    -- initial subscription, so a write may stamp that identity.
    --
    -- THIS RUN'S HANDSHAKE, NOT THE STORED OWNER. core/Host.owner can predate
    -- this run, and a board must not stamp a name it has not proved on this
    -- run's connection. Set once the first connection's initial subscription
    -- is applied and never cleared for the rest of the run: a dropped
    -- connection does not change the person, and a write during the outage is
    -- refused by AWriteWithNoConnectionIsRefused anyway.
    is_identified: identity_settled_this_run()

    transitions status {
        connecting -> connected
        connecting -> disconnected
        connected -> disconnected
        connected -> failed
        disconnected -> connecting
        terminal: failed

        -- THERE IS NO `connecting -> failed` EDGE, and its absence is a claim
        -- rather than an omission. The only thing that reaches `failed` is an
        -- identity conflict, and a conflict is discovered downstream of
        -- acceptance — a store that has not accepted the connection has
        -- offered no identity for anyone to disagree with. An edge here would
        -- be one no rule witnesses, which is to say a state a reader could
        -- reach in their head and the system never in fact enters.
    }

    -- One board, one connection. Two would mean two subscription sets and two
    -- retry clocks against one store, and the second one would be invisible:
    -- nothing an operator sees distinguishes one healthy connection from one
    -- healthy connection beside a thrashing one.
    invariant AtMostOneBoardConnection {
        BoardConnections.count <= 1
    }
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    -- The wait before the first retry after a failed attempt. Doubles per
    -- consecutive attempt up to the ceiling below.
    --
    -- Short on purpose. The overwhelmingly common outage is a few seconds long
    -- — a lid, a tunnel, a wifi handover — and a board that waits half a minute
    -- to notice the network came back is a board that feels broken for half a
    -- minute after it is fixed.
    reconnect_backoff_base: Duration = 1.second

    -- The longest the board will ever wait between attempts.
    --
    -- The ceiling exists for the other outage: the store is down for an hour,
    -- and an unbounded backoff would have the board checking once a day by the
    -- time somebody fixes it. A minute is short enough that a person who
    -- repaired the store sees the board recover while still looking at it.
    reconnect_backoff_max: Duration = 60.seconds

    -- How long one step of an attempt may hang before the attempt counts as
    -- failed. An attempt has four steps, each bounded on its own: opening the
    -- connection, the store's identity answer, the rules-version read
    -- (AStoreWithOtherRulesFailsTheAttempt) and the initial subscription.
    --
    -- Needed because the interesting failure is not refusal, it is silence: a
    -- host that accepts a connection and never answers leaves a board in
    -- `connecting` forever, which reports as "starting up" rather than as the
    -- outage it is. Without this the state machine has no edge out of that.
    connect_timeout: Duration = 10.seconds

    -- How long one mutation may wait for the store's answer.
    --
    -- The same argument as connect_timeout, one layer in. A store that accepts
    -- a change and never answers would otherwise leave the caller waiting
    -- forever, and nothing else would break the wait: a drop is noticed only if
    -- the transport closes, and a store that has stopped answering need not
    -- have.
    --
    -- Shorter than connect_timeout deliberately. A connection is set up once
    -- and may cross a slow link; a mutation happens while somebody is looking
    -- at the screen.
    --
    -- EXPIRY IS NOT A REFUSAL. The change was sent, so it may have been
    -- applied — see the open question about a write whose fate is unknown.
    mutation_timeout: Duration = 5.seconds

    -- Status values a row may still carry in an older store but that this
    -- binary does not have. A task or epic row whose raw status text is on
    -- this list is dropped wherever it is read from a store — see
    -- RowsWithARemovedStatusAreDropped. `archived` is the only one, and the
    -- only removed value seen in a real old store.
    --
    -- A LIST OF WHAT WAS REMOVED, NOT OF WHAT IS UNKNOWN. A status that is
    -- neither current nor on this list is still a row this binary cannot
    -- understand, and is refused the way any undecodable row is. Growing this
    -- list is a decision taken when a status is removed, never a way to quiet
    -- a warning.
    removed_statuses: Set<String> = {"archived"}

    -- The version of the data rules this build expects the store to enforce.
    -- Compared for EQUALITY with the rules version the store's module
    -- publishes; see AStoreWithOtherRulesFailsTheAttempt.
    --
    -- THE NUMBER LIVES IN THE CODE, NOT HERE. The module holds it as
    -- spacetime/module/src/rules.rs::RULES_VERSION and the board as
    -- src/sync/rules_gate.rs::BOARD_RULES_VERSION (the board's release build
    -- does not link the module crate); a test fails when the two differ, so
    -- both are bumped together, by hand. What does and does not warrant a
    -- bump is defined once, in RULES_VERSION's doc comment. Neither the
    -- module's wasm hash nor the board's build version is compared: a hash
    -- changes on every rebuild, and a build version would need a stored
    -- minimum that somebody also has to bump.
    rules_version: Integer = board_rules_version()
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- == Coming up ==

rule OpenBoardConnection {
    when: startup/StoreConnectionAttemptedAtStartup(operator)

    requires:
        configured_store_server() != null
        and BoardConnections.count = 0

    ensures:
        BoardConnection.created(
            server: configured_store_server(),
            status: connecting,
            attempts: 0,
            board_drawn: false
        )

    @guidance
        -- THE BOARD IS NOT YET DRAWN WHEN THIS FIRES, and it waits. startup.allium
        -- fires this once the host is named, and draws only when
        -- FirstConnectionLetsTheBoardDraw answers; a first attempt that fails
        -- aborts the launch instead (FirstConnectionFailureAbortsStartup).
        --
        -- CONNECT FIRST, DRAW AFTER. There are no local rows: a board drawn
        -- before its first connection would be empty, would refuse every
        -- change (AWriteWithNoConnectionIsRefused), and would have read its
        -- settings and repo paths from nothing. What is bought is a board that
        -- is right the moment it appears. What is paid is one round trip per
        -- cold start, bounded by config.connect_timeout, and no offline start.
        --
        -- ONLY THE FIRST ATTEMPT WAITS. Once the board has drawn, a dropped
        -- connection is the ordinary outage the rest of this file describes:
        -- disconnected, backing off, retried, drawn empty meanwhile.
        --
        -- The count guard makes a second board start in the same process a
        -- no-op rather than a second connection — see AtMostOneBoardConnection
        -- for why two would be worse than visibly broken.
}

rule FirstConnectionLetsTheBoardDraw {
    when: InitialSubscriptionApplied(connection)

    -- The operator is identified by this machine's host (core.allium:
    -- Operator); the startup events below carry it on to startup.allium.
    let operator = core/local_host()

    requires: not connection.board_drawn

    ensures:
        connection.board_drawn = true
        StoreReachedAtStartup(operator)

    @guidance
        -- APPLIED, not merely accepted. The identity has settled and the
        -- initial subscription's rows have arrived (SubscribeOnceIdentityIsSettled),
        -- so every startup read of a shared row finds what the store holds.
        -- Epics followed by Subscription rows arrive one round trip later
        -- (the covered_epics view) and are not waited for: they come in
        -- live, the way a teammate's edit does.
}

rule FirstConnectionFailureAbortsStartup {
    when: ConnectionAttemptFailed(connection, reason)
        or InitialSubscriptionRefused(connection, reason)

    let operator = core/local_host()

    requires: not connection.board_drawn

    ensures: StoreUnreachableAtStartup(operator, reason)

    @guidance
        -- The first attempt's failure is not an outage to wait out: the board
        -- has never been up. The launch aborts with the reason
        -- (startup.allium: AbortWhenTheStoreCannotBeReached), and
        -- ConnectionAttemptFailed's backoff never starts. Every failure mode
        -- ConnectionAttemptFailed covers lands here the same way, and so does a
        -- subscription the store refuses on that first connection
        -- (InitialSubscriptionRefused; after the board has drawn, the same
        -- event is ASubscriptionTheStoreRefusesIsAnOutage's).
}

rule IdentityConflictAtStartupAbortsIt {
    when: host/UserIdentityConflict(stored, offered)

    let connection = the_board_connection()
    let operator = core/local_host()

    requires: not connection.board_drawn

    ensures: StoreUnreachableAtStartup(operator, identity_conflict_message(stored, offered))

    @guidance
        -- StopOnAUserIdentityConflict still moves the connection to `failed`;
        -- this adds only that a board which has not drawn yet does not draw.
        -- The message is the same composed one the indicator would show.
}

rule ConnectionAccepted {
    when: ConnectionAccepted(connection, identity)

    requires: connection.status = connecting

    ensures:
        connection.status = connected
        connection.attempts = 0
        UserIdentityOffered(identity: identity)

    @guidance
        -- The store accepted the connection and told us who it thinks we are.
        -- That answer is not evaluated here: it goes to host.allium, which owns
        -- both the stored identity and the decision about a changed one, and
        -- comes back as UserIdentitySettled or UserIdentityConflict.
        --
        -- The split is deliberate and it is not ceremony. "Is this the same
        -- person?" is a question about identity, and answering it beside the
        -- socket is how a retry loop ends up with an opinion about who you are.
        --
        -- `attempts` resets here rather than on the next failure, so the backoff
        -- of a NEW outage starts short. A counter that survived a successful
        -- connection would punish a board for an outage that is over.
        --
        -- The identity ARRIVES WITH THE ACCEPTANCE rather than being fetched
        -- afterwards. A store that accepted a connection without saying who it
        -- thinks you are would leave a window in which the board is up and
        -- nobody — and every rule downstream would need an arm for it.
}

rule SubscribeOnceIdentityIsSettled {
    when: host/UserIdentitySettled(identity)

    let connection = the_board_connection()

    requires: connection.status = connected

    ensures:
        SubscriptionsAsserted(
            connection: connection,
            owner_board: identity,
            views: identity
        )

    @guidance
        -- NOTHING SYNCS BEFORE THE IDENTITY IS SETTLED, and the ordering is the
        -- point of having a separate rule. A board that subscribed on connect
        -- and settled its identity afterwards would, in the conflict case,
        -- already have asked for a stranger's rows before discovering it was a
        -- stranger.
        --
        -- THE BOARD ASKS THE VIEWS, NOT THE TABLES. Every table that carries a
        -- person's work is private (docs/architecture.md, "Read enforcement:
        -- per-caller views"), and the board subscribes to the per-caller views
        -- over them, which answer for the connection's own identity:
        --   own_tasks, own_epics, own_packages: what this person created,
        --     and the epic-less tasks they own (own_creations); own_tasks
        --     also the tasks run on a host this person owns (a current
        --     agent, or an open dispatch or session request, there), while
        --     they still see them: the host's reactor
        --     acts on the task a request row names, so the task must
        --     arrive with the row, and its subagent rows with it;
        --   covered_epics, covered_tasks: the epics this person follows and
        --     every descendant, at any depth, and their tasks;
        --   rows about a held task (learning retrievals and verdicts, unseen
        --     marks, bookmarks),
        --     agents and dispatch and session requests (a held task's, plus
        --     every row on a host this person owns, so a request outlives
        --     the person's sight of its task: unfollowing the epic of a
        --     teammate's task one dispatched must not lose the wrap-up row
        --     or its serving); feed requests (store-split.allium:
        --     FeedRequest), which concern no held task, only on the hosts
        --     this person owns; and the links, poll claims,
        --     learnings, settings, workspaces and memberships the caller
        --     may see, and the super-admin's unseen-item listing.
        -- Usage is not a view: the board asks the store on demand
        -- (own_usage_summary). Public tables (hosts, user_profiles, repo
        -- lists) are asked for directly. This person's own follows come
        -- through the own_subscriptions view; the subscriptions table is
        -- private, so no client can read another person's follows.
        --
        -- THE STORE WALKS THE TREE, THE BOARD DOES NOT. covered_epics is
        -- derived from the Subscription rows on the store's side, so a
        -- follow, an unfollow, a new sub-epic and a reparent change what the
        -- board receives live, with no widening and no reassert.
        --
        -- Four asks stay narrowed by host, because a view serves every host
        -- this person owns and a request or setting is one machine's:
        -- own_settings, visible_dispatch_requests, visible_session_requests
        -- and visible_feed_requests carry WHERE host = this host, in every
        -- state, so a row never leaves a state-filtered query as a delete.
        --
        -- WHY own_creations EXISTS, AND WHY IT IS UNCONDITIONAL. A reducer
        -- cannot return a value (see EveryMutationIsAtomicAndAnswered's
        -- "a reply, not only an effect" clause), so the only way this board
        -- ever learns the id a create was given is by already being subscribed
        -- to a query that covers the new row once the store applies it — see
        -- CreatesRequireASettledIdentity below. own_tasks, own_epics and
        -- own_packages cover a brand-new epic and a task created into an epic
        -- nobody here follows, by who created them.
        --
        -- A view's rows arrive with the transaction that wrote them, so a
        -- create's callback finds its row there. Live-store test:
        -- tests/spacetime_module (generated id read back through the views).
        --
        -- RE-ASSERTED ON EVERY CONNECTION, unconditionally, including the
        -- reconnects where nothing changed. Subscriptions are per-connection
        -- and a dropped connection takes them with it; re-subscribing is
        -- idempotent by core/Subscription's uniqueness invariant, so the cost of
        -- asserting an unchanged set is a round trip and the cost of assuming it
        -- survived is a board that silently stops updating.
}

rule SubscribeToThisHostsSettings {
    when: host/UserIdentitySettled(identity)

    let connection = the_board_connection()

    requires: connection.status = connected

    ensures:
        SettingsSubscriptionAsserted(
            connection: connection,
            host: core/local_host()
        )

    @guidance
        -- THE SETTINGS ASK, SCOPED BY MACHINE. Beside the asks
        -- SubscribeOnceIdentityIsSettled makes by person, the board asks for
        -- this host's own core/Setting rows and no other host's
        -- (settings.allium: SettingsAreStoredPerHost). The host id is known
        -- before any identity settles — it comes from the host file — but the
        -- ask is asserted with the others so that one initial subscription,
        -- applied once, delivers everything the board reads at startup
        -- (FirstConnectionLetsTheBoardDraw), settings included.
        --
        -- It does not weaken SubscriptionsCoverOnlyTheOwnBoardAndItsEpics'
        -- containment claim: what it names is this machine's own rows, so a
        -- colleague's settings — their filters, their feed scripts' paths —
        -- are never on this machine. Re-asserted on every connection, like
        -- the rest.
        --
        -- The settings are read from these rows and from nowhere else; there
        -- is no local copy to fall back to while the store is down, and a
        -- disconnected board reads none (ADisconnectedBoardDrawsNoSharedRows).
}

rule SubscribeToThisHostsDispatchRequests {
    when: host/UserIdentitySettled(identity)

    let connection = the_board_connection()

    requires: connection.status = connected

    ensures:
        DispatchRequestSubscriptionAsserted(
            connection: connection,
            host: core/local_host()
        )

    @guidance
        -- THE DISPATCH-REQUEST ASK, scoped by machine like the settings ask: every
        -- store-split.allium DispatchRequest row whose host is this machine,
        -- in every state. A request's host is always the machine that asked
        -- for it, so this delivers both the work this host must provision
        -- (store-split.allium: HostClaimsDispatch picks up requested rows,
        -- including the initial rows a starting board receives) and the
        -- outcome of every request this host made, which DispatchingFeedback
        -- and dispatch_task read. Finished rows are included because a row
        -- leaving a state-filtered query would arrive as a delete, and the
        -- outcome would be lost.
        --
        -- It does not weaken SubscriptionsCoverOnlyTheOwnBoardAndItsEpics:
        -- the rows are this machine's own. Re-asserted on every connection,
        -- like the rest.
}

rule SubscribeToThisHostsSessionRequests {
    when: host/UserIdentitySettled(identity)

    let connection = the_board_connection()

    requires: connection.status = connected

    ensures:
        SessionRequestSubscriptionAsserted(
            connection: connection,
            host: core/local_host()
        )

    @guidance
        -- THE SESSION-REQUEST ASK, scoped by machine like the dispatch-request
        -- ask and asserted beside it: every store-split.allium SessionRequest
        -- row whose host is this machine, in every state. A session request
        -- names the host holding the task's worktree and window, which is the
        -- machine whose board the task's agent talks to, so this delivers the
        -- work this host must do (store-split.allium:
        -- HostClaimsSessionRequest picks up requested rows, including the
        -- initial rows a starting board receives), the outcome the wrap_up
        -- MCP tool waits on, the chained_task exit_session's response names,
        -- and the exit_session rows exit_session's no-answer settling reads
        -- (mcp-task-tools.allium: WrapUpViaMcp, ExitSessionViaMcp). It also
        -- delivers the close rows of update_tasks'
        -- done path (store-split.allium: RequestCloseTask), which this host
        -- tears down like an exit_session row. The
        -- token check itself is the module's, over its own table
        -- (store-split.allium: RequestExitSession), not over these rows. Finished
        -- rows are included for the same reason as the dispatch-request ask: a row
        -- leaving a state-filtered query would arrive as a delete, and the
        -- outcome would be lost.
        --
        -- It does not weaken SubscriptionsCoverOnlyTheOwnBoardAndItsEpics:
        -- the rows are this machine's own. Re-asserted on every connection,
        -- like the rest.
}

rule SubscribeToThisHostsFeedRequests {
    when: host/UserIdentitySettled(identity)

    let connection = the_board_connection()

    requires: connection.status = connected

    ensures:
        FeedRequestSubscriptionAsserted(
            connection: connection,
            host: core/local_host()
        )

    @guidance
        -- THE FEED-REQUEST ASK, scoped by machine like the dispatch- and
        -- session-request asks and asserted beside them: every store-split.allium
        -- FeedRequest row whose host is this machine, in every state, read
        -- through the visible_feed_requests view. The view answers a caller
        -- with the rows on the hosts its identity owns; the ask narrows that
        -- to this host, and the board acts only on its own host's rows. A
        -- refresh row names the epic's poll owner and a teardown row the
        -- host holding a removed feed task, so this delivers the work this
        -- host must do (feeds.allium: FeedTick picks the open rows up,
        -- including those a starting board receives in its initial rows, and
        -- ServeFeedRefreshRequest and ServeFeedTeardownRequest serve them).
        -- Finished rows are included for the same reason as the dispatch-request ask: a
        -- row leaving a state-filtered query would arrive as a delete.
        --
        -- Unlike the dispatch and session asks, a feed request concerns no
        -- task this person can see (a teardown's task is already deleted), so
        -- there is no held-task leg: only the owned-hosts one.
        --
        -- It does not weaken SubscriptionsCoverOnlyTheOwnBoardAndItsEpics:
        -- the rows are this machine's own. Re-asserted on every connection,
        -- like the rest.
}

rule SubscribeToUserProfiles {
    when: host/UserIdentitySettled(identity)

    let connection = the_board_connection()

    requires: connection.status = connected

    ensures: UserProfileSubscriptionAsserted(connection: connection)

    @guidance
        -- Every UserProfile row (workspaces.allium), unfiltered: the
        -- directory of identities and display names is readable by every
        -- connected client (workspaces.allium, D7). A row holds an identity,
        -- a name, the super-admin flag and a creation stamp, nothing private.
        -- The board needs it to show a creator by name
        -- (CreatorIsShownByName). Re-asserted on every connection, like the
        -- rest.
}

rule SubscribeToAgents {
    when: host/UserIdentitySettled(identity)

    let connection = the_board_connection()

    requires: connection.status = connected

    ensures: AgentSubscriptionAsserted(connection: connection)

    @guidance
        -- THE AGENTS ASK (agents.allium, A1): the Agent rows the
        -- visible_agents view serves this identity — every agent, ended ones
        -- too, of a task the caller holds, and every agent on a host the
        -- caller owns. The board needs ended agents as well as current ones,
        -- because A2 resolves a name to its task on any task the board can
        -- see. The owned-hosts leg keeps an agent this host runs on its board
        -- after its task has left the caller's sight. The retired name ledger
        -- is NOT asked for: nothing reads it (agents.allium: A4).
        -- Re-asserted on every connection, like the rest.
        --
        -- A4: the agent row also carries the live-session facts (window, hook
        -- timestamps, subagent count, deferred Stop), and the stored task row
        -- carries none of them. The board lays a task's current agent over the
        -- task it hands out (models::with_session_of, applied in SharedRows),
        -- so everything that reads a task's window or hook stamps reads the
        -- agent through the task. A task whose agent row has not arrived yet
        -- reads as having no session until it does.
}

-- A row served through a per-caller view has no primary key in the generated
-- bindings (docs/architecture.md, "Read enforcement: per-caller views"). The
-- store's update of such a row reaches the board as an insert of the new row
-- and a delete of the old
-- one, with the same id, in either order.
rule AViewUpdateNeverErasesTheRow {
    when: ViewRowUpdated(connection, row_old, row_new)

    requires: row_old.id = row_new.id

    ensures: connection.held(row_new.id) = row_new

    @guidance
        -- THE DELETE ONLY REMOVES THE ROW IT NAMES. The board removes an id
        -- on a retraction only while the row it holds equals the retracted
        -- one (SharedRows::retire_task, retire_epic). A retraction of a row
        -- the board no longer holds is the tail of an update and changes
        -- nothing. "Equals" is judged on the raw store row, not the decoded
        -- one: rows that differ only in a column the decoder ignores (such
        -- as pr_learnings_gate_shown_at) must still count as different, or
        -- the delete half of that update erases the row and every read of
        -- the task answers "not found" until it is next written.
        --
        -- EVERY TABLE THAT MOVES BEHIND A VIEW takes this path, not just
        -- tasks and epics. This rule is built for those two first; each
        -- later table adds its own retire method, and a view table is wired
        -- with on_insert and on_delete only (no on_update exists for it).
        -- `wire_view!` (sdk_connector/wiring.rs) is that wiring: a view's
        -- delete goes to the table's retire method, never to its remove.
        --
        -- A ROW IN TWO VIEWS IS HELD UNTIL IT HAS LEFT BOTH. A task or epic
        -- the caller created and also covers is served by own_tasks and
        -- covered_tasks (own_epics and covered_epics), and leaves one of them
        -- without leaving the other: an unfollow, or a task moved out of a
        -- followed epic. The board removes the id only when the other view's
        -- cache no longer holds it (sdk_connector/wiring.rs,
        -- `wire_board_views`), on top of the equality check above.
}

rule RegisterHostOnConnect {
    when: host/UserIdentitySettled(identity)

    let connection = the_board_connection()

    requires: connection.status = connected

    ensures:
        HostRegistrationSentToStore(connection, core/local_host())

    @guidance
        -- THIS MACHINE'S ROW ALREADY EXISTS. host.allium: MintHostIdentity
        -- creates it, offline, before any connection is attempted. What does
        -- not yet exist, the first time this rule fires, is the STORE's copy
        -- of it — core/Hosts is a registry the moment a second machine shares
        -- a store (core/Host's ExactlyOneLocalHost commentary already says
        -- so), and a row that only ever reached this machine's own disk is
        -- not one a colleague's board can look up. This rule is what makes
        -- that copy exist; it is not what makes the row exist. Contrast
        -- BoardWritesThroughTheStore, which sends a row whose creation and
        -- transmission are the same act — this is the one row in the system
        -- whose local write happens before any connection does, so sending it
        -- is a catch-up rather than a forwarding.
        --
        -- FIRES ON EVERY SETTLE, RECONNECTS INCLUDED, for the same reason
        -- SubscribeOnceIdentityIsSettled re-asserts subscriptions
        -- unconditionally: a dropped connection does not tell this rule
        -- whether the store's copy of this row is still current — a rename
        -- while disconnected is exactly that case — and the cheap answer is
        -- to re-send it rather than track staleness.
        --
        -- BEST-EFFORT, LIKE EVERY WRITE MutationSentToStore ALREADY COVERS.
        -- A failed or delayed send here does not block the connection and
        -- does not block the board drawing. The worst outcome is a stale or
        -- absent registry row on a machine that is, itself, working fine —
        -- the same bargain BoardWritesThroughTheStore already makes for
        -- every shared write, not a weaker one carved out for this rule.
        --
        -- WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO: make the label live-synced. A colleague's
        -- board redrawing when this row changes is an ordinary consequence of
        -- SubscribedRowsArriveUnasked, once this rule has sent a row for it to
        -- arrive from — this rule's job stops at getting the write to the
        -- store the first time.
        --
        -- ALWAYS AS THE SETTLED IDENTITY, NEVER OWNERLESS. The row is sent
        -- with the identity this rule's trigger settled as its owner, and a
        -- connection with no settled identity sends nothing — it waits for
        -- the settle rather than register the host without an owner. The
        -- store refuses a NEW ownerless host anyway (core/Host's
        -- LocalHostOwnerIsWrittenOnce; store-split.allium, D11): an ownerless
        -- row would be open to whoever registered its public id first.
        --
        -- SENT ONCE THE CONNECTION IS ALL THE WAY UP, not at the instant the
        -- identity settles: after the initial subscription has been applied.
        -- A connection whose initial subscription is refused
        -- (ASubscriptionTheStoreRefusesIsAnOutage) sends no registration; the
        -- next connection that does come up sends it.
}

rule RegisterHostOnRename {
    when: host/HostRenamed()

    let connection = the_board_connection()

    requires: connection.status = connected

    ensures:
        HostRegistrationSentToStore(connection, core/local_host())

    @guidance
        -- THE SAME SEND AS RegisterHostOnConnect, fired by a rename instead of
        -- a connection settling, so a relabel while already connected does not
        -- wait for a reconnect that may never come. Reads
        -- `core/local_host()` AFTER host.allium: RenameHost's own write, since
        -- ensures-block reads see the resulting state — see that rule for the
        -- write this mirrors.
        --
        -- REQUIRES A CONNECTION AND DOES NOTHING WITHOUT ONE. A rename while
        -- disconnected is fully applied locally by host.allium: RenameHost
        -- regardless — that rule does not consult this one — and the stale
        -- registry copy is caught up by RegisterHostOnConnect on whatever
        -- connection comes next. There is no queued retry here: a mirror this
        -- best-effort does not need a second mechanism for the case its first
        -- one already covers.
        --
        -- The startup prompt names the host before the first connection, so
        -- that rename always takes this second path: the guard fails and
        -- RegisterHostOnConnect sends the row.
}

-- == What the board draws ==

rule SubscribedRowsArriveUnasked {
    when: StoreDeliversRow(connection, row)

    requires: connection.status = connected
    requires: not (is_task_or_epic_row(row) and row_status(row) in config.removed_statuses)

    ensures:
        SubscribedRowAccepted(connection, row)
        and BoardRedrawn()

    @guidance
        -- THIS IS WHAT REPLACES POLLING, and it is the visible difference a
        -- shared board makes. A colleague moves a card on their machine and it
        -- moves here — not within a refresh interval, but as the row arrives.
        --
        -- The redraw reads the WHOLE board rather than applying the row as a
        -- delta. The delta is the store's bookkeeping and reconstructing it
        -- here would be a second copy of it, free to drift; re-reading what is
        -- already in memory costs nothing worth saving it over.
        --
        -- A periodic refresh MAY still exist as a backstop and is not a
        -- contradiction: it changes nothing when nothing has changed. What
        -- would be a contradiction is a board that depends on it, because that
        -- board is one whose colleague's work is always up to one interval old.
}

rule ADisconnectedBoardDrawsNoSharedRows {
    when: ConnectionDropped(connection, reason)

    ensures:
        SubscribedRowsDiscarded(connection)

    @guidance
        -- The rows belonged to that connection. Subscriptions are
        -- per-connection (SubscribeOnceIdentityIsSettled says so), the next one
        -- re-delivers from scratch, and rows kept across the gap would be a
        -- board's contents on screen with nothing live behind them — arrived at
        -- by accident rather than chosen, which is the worst way to have a
        -- cache.
        --
        -- SO A DISCONNECTED BOARD IS AN EMPTY BOARD, and that is accepted
        -- rather than regretted. It is also why ConnectionIndicator's
        -- DisconnectedIsAlwaysVisible is not a nicety: without it, "the store
        -- is down" and "you have nothing to do" look identical.
        --
        -- The reconnect is ordinary and fast (config.reconnect_backoff_base is
        -- a second), so the state this describes is normally a blink. It is
        -- specified because the outage that is NOT a blink is the one somebody
        -- has to understand.
}

rule RowsWithARemovedStatusAreDropped {
    when: StoreDeliversRow(connection, row) or seed/RowReadForImport(row)

    -- Only tasks and epics: other tables have their own statuses (a
    -- learning may be `archived`, and that is a current status of its own).
    requires: is_task_or_epic_row(row)
    requires: row_status(row) in config.removed_statuses

    ensures:
        RowDropped(row)

    @guidance
        -- ONE PREDICATE, TWO READERS. The board's subscription decode (a row
        -- the store delivers) and the store-import task (a row read from the
        -- old shared store to be brought into the managed one) ask the same
        -- question of the same thing: is this row's raw status text on
        -- config.removed_statuses? It is asked of the raw text, before the row
        -- is decoded, because a removed status has no value in this binary to
        -- decode to. Two copies of the question would be two lists free to
        -- disagree about which rows exist.
        --
        -- DROPPED MEANS GONE, NOTHING ELSE. The row is not imported, not
        -- drawn, not mapped to `done` or any other current status, and not
        -- kept as a retired item. No history of it is kept anywhere. The old
        -- store holds roughly 1,790 archived tasks and 165 archived epics;
        -- each is work somebody already put away, and none is worth a status
        -- it never had.
        --
        -- THE ACCEPTED COST: A FEED MAY BRING ONE BACK. An archived feed task
        -- is not recorded as a core/RetiredFeedItem, so nothing tells its feed
        -- that the item was retired, and the feed's next poll may create it
        -- afresh (feeds.allium). That is accepted: a re-created item is one
        -- card the operator can delete again, and deleting it then retires it
        -- the ordinary way.
        --
        -- NOT AN ERROR, AND NOT COUNTED. A dropped row is skipped silently:
        -- the board logs it at debug level only, an import counts it in its
        -- report, and nothing is ever logged at warn.
        -- It does NOT emit observability.allium's DecodeFallbackWarning and
        -- does not bump its counter, which exists to show rows that really
        -- could not be decoded — two thousand expected drops would bury the one
        -- that matters.
        --
        -- AN UNKNOWN STATUS IS STILL REFUSED. A status that is neither a
        -- current one nor on config.removed_statuses is not this rule's: it
        -- stays an undecodable row, warned and counted as before
        -- (observability.allium: BulkRowDecodeFailed), and dropped from the
        -- board as AConnectedBoardDrawsOnlyDeliveredRows says.
        --
        -- SubscribedRowsArriveUnasked carries the converse guard, so a
        -- delivered row is either accepted there or dropped here, never both.
}

-- == What the board writes ==

rule BoardWritesThroughTheStore {
    when: SharedRowMutationRequested(mutation)

    let connection = the_board_connection()

    requires:
        connection != null
        and connection.status = connected

    ensures:
        MutationSentToStore(connection, mutation)

    @guidance
        -- THE MIRROR OF AConnectedBoardDrawsOnlyDeliveredRows, and it has to be, or the
        -- two halves disagree about which copy is real. A board that read the
        -- store and wrote its own disk would make every change it performed
        -- invisible to itself the moment it was made: written to a copy nothing
        -- draws from, absent from the copy everything draws from, and reported
        -- as a success.
        --
        -- ONE PATH. Every drawn board has a connection, so there is no local
        -- branch for a shared write to take instead. A store that receives
        -- only some of a board's shared writes is the one state worse than no
        -- store at all: it looks fine while the two copies drift apart, so no
        -- shared write may ever be left on a path that skips the store.
        --
        -- NOT EVERY MUTATION IN THE SYSTEM — every mutation of a SHARED row,
        -- which includes this host's settings, the knowledge base with its embeddings
        -- and retrievals, and usage telemetry. What stays local is this
        -- install's own identity: its Host row (id and label, mirrored to the
        -- shared registry by RegisterHostOnConnect) and the stored user
        -- identity with the credential that proves it, all in the host file
        -- (host.allium: IdentityLivesInHostFile). The line is core.allium's,
        -- not this file's.
        --
        -- AND NOT ANYTHING THAT RUNS A PROCESS. Dispatching an agent,
        -- provisioning a worktree, releasing one, running a feed's command:
        -- those happen on the machine the operator is sitting at, because a
        -- store has no terminal, no checkout and no git. What reaches the store
        -- is the ROW that records the outcome, and the order matters — the
        -- worktree exists before the row that names it does.
        --
        -- WHOSE CLOCK. A time that records when something happened on this
        -- machine is this board's: a row's creation instant, and the instant
        -- an agent's hook fired. Every time the store derives as a consequence
        -- of a write — a last-updated stamp, a completion time — is the
        -- store's, so that rows written by different boards are ordered by one
        -- clock rather than by whose laptop runs fast.
}

rule CreatesRequireASettledIdentity {
    when: SharedRowMutationRequested(mutation)

    let connection = the_board_connection()

    requires:
        connection != null
        and mutation.is_a_create
        and not mutation.is_feed_ingest
        and not connection.is_identified

    ensures:
        MutationRefused(connection, mutation, reason: "this board has no settled identity yet, so there is no name to stamp on a new row")
        and not MutationSentToStore(connection, mutation)

    @guidance
        -- THE THIRD THING A CREATE NEEDS, BESIDES A CONNECTION. A create is a
        -- SharedRowMutationRequested like any other, but unlike a patch or a
        -- delete it has to stamp a name on the row it is about to make —
        -- core/Task.owner or core/Task.created_by, core/Epic.created_by —
        -- and there is no placeholder for "whoever
        -- this turns out to be". A connected-but-not-yet-identified board can
        -- send a patch or a delete (neither names anyone), but a create has
        -- nothing honest to write in that field, so it is refused here instead
        -- of writing an empty one.
        --
        -- FEED INGEST IS THE ONE CREATE THAT DOES NOT WAIT. A feed's tasks
        -- are the feed's, not a person's: ingest stamps an empty created_by
        -- when no identity is settled (feeds.allium: IngestFeedEmission), so
        -- there is no name it could get wrong.
        --
        -- NOT GUARDED ON connection.status = connected, DELIBERATELY. An
        -- unidentified board and a disconnected board overlap: identity settles
        -- only after a successful connection, so a create attempted before the
        -- first connection completes is both unidentified and not yet
        -- connected at once. This rule and AWriteWithNoConnectionIsRefused are
        -- both true of that create, and either refusal is honest — nothing is
        -- silently accepted either way. Once identity IS settled it survives a
        -- later drop (`host.allium`'s identity is never cleared once adopted),
        -- so a create refused for a stale connection after that point is
        -- AWriteWithNoConnectionIsRefused's alone, and this rule does not
        -- refire for it.
        --
        -- THIS IS WHAT MAKES own_creations RELIABLE, not an unrelated
        -- tightening. SubscribeOnceIdentityIsSettled asserts own_creations the
        -- moment identity settles, in the same rule firing as the settlement
        -- itself. So "identity is settled" and "own_creations is already
        -- asked for" become true together, and a create that waits for the
        -- former is guaranteed the latter already holds by the time the store
        -- answers it — which is the whole reason a create can read back the
        -- id it was given.
        --
        -- ONLY CREATES WAIT FOR AN IDENTITY. Every other mutation (a patch, a
        -- delete, a claim, an unsubscribe) stamps nobody's name, so none of
        -- them waits on anything past a connected transport. The window
        -- between acceptance and the identity verdict is real for them too,
        -- but harmless: nothing in the written row could later read as
        -- belonging to the wrong person.
}

rule AWriteWithNoConnectionIsRefused {
    when: SharedRowMutationRequested(mutation)

    let connection = the_board_connection()

    requires:
        connection != null
        and connection.status != connected

    ensures:
        MutationRefused(connection, mutation, reason: connection.last_error)
        and not MutationSentToStore(connection, mutation)

    @guidance
        -- IT FAILS, LOUDLY, AND NOTHING ANYWHERE CHANGES. Not queued, not
        -- retried, not written to the local copy as a stand-in. The caller
        -- learns it did not happen, at the moment it did not happen, and the
        -- operator sees the refusal rather than discovering it later.
        --
        -- WHY NOT A QUEUE, stated once so nobody has to relitigate it. A queued
        -- write is an intent formed against a board state that no longer holds
        -- by the time it lands. The operator moved a card to done because of
        -- what they could see; three minutes later a colleague has reassigned
        -- that task, and replaying the move writes a decision nobody would make
        -- now. Worse, the operator was told it worked and has moved on. A
        -- refusal is a smaller harm and an honest one: the person is still
        -- looking at the screen and can decide for themselves.
        --
        -- THE REFUSAL IS NOT THE OUTAGE REPORT. ConnectionIndicator already
        -- says the store is down, continuously; this says that THIS action did
        -- not happen. Both are needed — an indicator alone leaves a person
        -- guessing whether the thing they just pressed went through.
        --
        -- The reason is the connection's, because that is the true one. "The
        -- store is unreachable: connection refused" is actionable; "could not
        -- save" is not.
        --
        -- WHILE A RETRY IS IN FLIGHT THERE IS NO REASON TO QUOTE. RetryAfterBackoff
        -- clears last_error on the way into `connecting`, so a write refused
        -- during that attempt says only that the store is not connected and
        -- that nothing was changed or queued. It does not repeat the previous
        -- failure's reason as though it were current, and it does not invent
        -- one.
}

rule StoreRejectsAnInvalidMutation {
    when: StoreRejectedMutation(connection, mutation, reason)

    ensures:
        MutationRefused(connection, mutation, reason: reason)

    @guidance
        -- VALIDATION HAPPENS WHERE THE ROWS ARE. A rule about a shared row is a
        -- rule about the one copy of it, and a board can only ever check it
        -- against the subset it subscribes to — so a check performed here is a
        -- check performed against partial knowledge, and two boards holding
        -- different subsets would reach different verdicts about the same
        -- write. The store is the only party that sees all of it.
        --
        -- REJECTION IS ALL-OR-NOTHING. A mutation the store refuses leaves it
        -- exactly as it was, including any derived value the mutation would
        -- have triggered. A half-applied write is the one outcome no caller
        -- can recover from, because there is no way to ask what part landed.
        --
        -- The reason travels back and is shown. A store that refused writes
        -- without saying why would turn every validation rule into a board that
        -- mysteriously does nothing.
        --
        -- The board does NOT pre-check to save a round trip. A duplicated
        -- validator is a validator free to disagree with the real one, and the
        -- disagreement surfaces as either a refusal the store would have
        -- allowed or, far worse, a confident local "yes" the store then refuses
        -- anyway. One authority.
}

rule AStoreWithOtherRulesFailsTheAttempt {
    when: StoreRulesVersionRead(connection, store_rules_version)

    requires: connection.status = connecting
    requires: store_rules_version != config.rules_version

    ensures:
        ConnectionAttemptFailed(
            connection,
            reason: rules_version_mismatch_message(store_rules_version, config.rules_version)
        )

    @guidance
        -- THE STARTUP VERSION GATE (store-split.allium). It
        -- runs on every connection attempt, once the store has answered the
        -- connection with an identity but before the board counts the attempt
        -- as accepted (ConnectionAccepted, and the identity handshake it
        -- starts), so a mismatched board never subscribes, draws or writes.
        -- The read is a one-shot subscription to the module's rules-version
        -- table, dropped again before the board subscribes for real. On the first attempt the failure aborts
        -- the launch (FirstConnectionFailureAbortsStartup); on a later one it
        -- is an ordinary outage with the reason on screen, so a module
        -- republished under a running board stops that board at its next
        -- reconnect rather than letting it carry on with the old rules.
        --
        -- BOTH DIRECTIONS, ONE COMPARISON. A board older than the module would
        -- apply rules the module no longer lets it apply, or skip rules the
        -- module now expects a host to follow. A board newer than the module
        -- would skip a rule it assumes the store enforces, and the half-moved
        -- state looks fine while it corrupts quietly. Equality refuses both.
        -- The message says which side is behind and what to do: update the
        -- board, or republish the module.
        --
        -- A module published before the gate existed has no rules-version
        -- table, so the store refuses the read itself. That too fails the
        -- attempt, with a reason naming the store's error, saying the module
        -- predates the gate and giving the advice for a version-0 module
        -- (republish). So does a read the store drops unanswered or that
        -- outlasts config.connect_timeout. A table with no row reads as 0,
        -- which no build expects, so a board refuses it too.
        --
        -- WHAT THE GATE CANNOT DO. A board built before this rule exists has no
        -- gate to refuse with, so it cannot be stopped from connecting to a
        -- newer module. The module's own checks still refuse what it can
        -- (an illegal sub_status, a cycle), with the reason travelling back
        -- (StoreRejectsAnInvalidMutation). That is "as far as possible".
}

-- == Staying up, and coming back ==

rule ConnectionAttemptFailed {
    when: ConnectionAttemptFailed(connection, reason)

    requires: connection.status = connecting
    requires: connection.board_drawn

    ensures:
        connection.status = disconnected
        connection.attempts = connection.attempts + 1
        connection.last_error = reason
        connection.last_failure_at = now

    @guidance
        -- Covers refusal, an unresolvable host, a rejected credential and the
        -- attempt that simply never answered within config.connect_timeout.
        -- They are one state because an operator's next action is the same for
        -- all of them — look at the reason — and the reason is carried, so
        -- nothing is lost by not branching.
        --
        -- NOT THE FIRST-START FAILURE. A board that has not drawn has nothing
        -- to draw while disconnected, so the first attempt's failure aborts
        -- the launch instead (FirstConnectionFailureAbortsStartup); this rule
        -- is every later one.
        --
        -- The counter increments here and nowhere else, which keeps the backoff
        -- reading one number that means one thing.
}

rule ConnectionDropped {
    when: ConnectionDropped(connection, reason)

    requires: connection.status = connected

    ensures:
        connection.status = disconnected
        connection.attempts = connection.attempts + 1
        connection.last_error = reason
        connection.last_failure_at = now

    @guidance
        -- A connection that WAS up and is no longer. Separate from
        -- ConnectionAttemptFailed because the two are reached from different
        -- states and the transition graph says so, but they land in the same
        -- place on purpose: from here on, recovery is identical, and a design
        -- with two flavours of disconnected would have two retry loops to keep
        -- in step.
        --
        -- THERE IS NO SUCH THING AS AN UNNOTICED DROP. A store that stops
        -- answering without closing anything is the normal case on a sleeping
        -- laptop, so a connection that only learns of drops from the far end is
        -- one that reports `connected` at a screen nobody is updating. What
        -- makes this trigger fire in that case is not specified here — it is a
        -- liveness question, not a domain one — but that it MUST fire is.
        --
        -- `attempts` increments here too, so an outage that begins from a
        -- healthy connection backs off exactly like one that begins from a
        -- failed attempt.
}

rule ASubscriptionTheStoreRefusesIsAnOutage {
    when: InitialSubscriptionRefused(connection, reason)

    requires: connection.status = connected
    requires: connection.board_drawn

    ensures:
        connection.status = disconnected
        connection.attempts = connection.attempts + 1
        connection.last_error = reason
        connection.last_failure_at = now
        SubscribedRowsDiscarded(connection)

    @guidance
        -- SUBSCRIBING IS PART OF COMING UP. The store accepted the connection
        -- and the identity settled, but the ask SubscribeOnceIdentityIsSettled
        -- sent was refused, or was not applied within config.connect_timeout.
        -- A connection that syncs nothing is not a working one, so it does not
        -- sit in `connected`: it becomes the same ordinary outage as a drop,
        -- with the store's reason as last_error, and RetryAfterBackoff brings
        -- it back.
        --
        -- CLOSED, NOT MERELY MARKED DOWN. The connection the store accepted is
        -- closed and whatever it had already delivered is discarded, exactly
        -- as ADisconnectedBoardDrawsNoSharedRows does for a drop. A connection
        -- left open would go on delivering rows into a board whose status
        -- says `disconnected`, and on accepting writes the board has just
        -- told the operator it will refuse.
        --
        -- The identity stays settled: the store did say who this is, and
        -- nothing about a refused ask changes that.
        --
        -- ONLY THE ASK MADE ON CONNECTING: the board makes no other.
}

rule RetryAfterBackoff {
    when: connection: BoardConnection.next_attempt_at <= now

    requires: connection.status = disconnected

    ensures:
        connection.status = connecting
        connection.last_error = null
        connection.last_failure_at = null

    @guidance
        -- The whole of the reconnect loop, and the reason this file exists at
        -- all: nothing under the board does this for us. A client library that
        -- reconnects on its own would make this rule redundant; one that does
        -- not makes it the difference between a board that recovers and a board
        -- that needs restarting after every wifi handover.
        --
        -- The delay is exponential from config.reconnect_backoff_base, doubling
        -- per consecutive attempt, capped at config.reconnect_backoff_max — see
        -- both for why each end of that range is where it is.
        --
        -- The `requires` is what stops it re-firing: the first thing the rule
        -- does is leave `disconnected`, so the temporal condition cannot hold
        -- again until a later failure puts it back.
        --
        -- IT DOES NOT FIRE FROM `failed`. That state is terminal in the graph
        -- above, and deliberately: retrying an identity conflict retries it
        -- forever, since nothing about a retry changes which person the store
        -- believes this is.
}

-- == Stopping ==

rule StopOnAUserIdentityConflict {
    when: host/UserIdentityConflict(stored, offered)

    let connection = the_board_connection()

    requires: connection.status = connected

    ensures:
        connection.status = failed
        connection.last_error = identity_conflict_message(stored, offered)

    @guidance
        -- The one fatal condition in this file. host.allium:
        -- RefuseAChangedUserIdentity has already refused the write and explained
        -- why; this is what refusing costs the board.
        --
        -- `failed` is terminal, so no retry follows and no subscription is
        -- asserted. A board in this state syncs nothing. Whether it also stops
        -- being a board at all is the difference between a cold start and a
        -- mid-session drop, and it is the same answer for both: the board does
        -- not continue to run as somebody. It reports the conflict, names both
        -- identities, and stops. Before the board has drawn that is
        -- IdentityConflictAtStartupAbortsIt. After, the board leaves its run
        -- loop the way a confirmed quit does (startup.allium: BoardExitCause
        -- quit), returning a pinned agent to its window first, and prints the
        -- message once the terminal is handed back, so it is still on screen
        -- after the board has gone. Not a dismissible popup over an empty
        -- board: a board that keeps running invites work it can no longer
        -- save.
        --
        -- The message is recorded as last_error before the board goes, so a
        -- write refused in the moment before it exits quotes the conflict
        -- (AWriteWithNoConnectionIsRefused), not a bare "not connected".
        --
        -- ONE STATE FOR BOTH ARRIVAL PATHS is what keeps that answer honest. The
        -- tempting design has a fatal cold start and a warning mid-session,
        -- because stopping a board somebody is working in feels rude. It is
        -- less rude than the alternative, which is continuing to accept work
        -- under an identity that owns none of it.
        --
        -- The `connected` guard is not a narrowing, it is the only state this
        -- can be reached from. The conflict is decided downstream of
        -- ConnectionAccepted, which moves the connection INTO `connected`, so a
        -- connection that is not up has offered no identity for anyone to
        -- disagree with. Written out rather than left implicit because it is
        -- what makes the `failed` edge one the transition graph above admits.
}

-- == Choosing what to follow ==

rule SubscribeToEpic {
    when: SubscribeToEpic(operator, epic)

    requires: core/local_host().owner != null

    ensures: core/Subscription.created(
        subscriber: core/local_host().owner,
        epic: epic
    )

    @guidance
        -- Following an epic means its tasks reach this person's board, on every
        -- machine they use, until they say otherwise. The row is the person's,
        -- not the machine's — which is why it is a shared Subscription rather
        -- than a local preference, and why subscribing on the laptop is already
        -- true on the desktop.
        --
        -- THE SUBSCRIBER IS THE CALLER. The store refuses a subscriber that is
        -- not the identity making the call, so a board cannot make someone
        -- else follow an epic (workspaces.allium, "Identities are the
        -- caller's"); the rule above already names the local host's owner.
        --
        -- REQUIRES AN IDENTITY, because a subscription is one person's standing
        -- interest and an install that has never connected does not know whose.
        -- This is the one place the null owner from core/Host bites, and it
        -- bites correctly: there is nothing to subscribe to a store this board
        -- has never reached.
        --
        -- IDEMPOTENT, by core/Subscription's SubscriptionIsUniquePerSubscriberAndEpic.
        -- Subscribing twice is subscribing; there is no error to report and
        -- nothing for a caller to check first.
        --
        -- THERE IS NO SubscribeToUserBoard, HERE OR ANYWHERE. Not refused — 
        -- absent. Your own board needs no row (the identity implies it) and
        -- somebody else's is theirs. An operation that could be called and
        -- refused would be an operation whose refusal could be got wrong.
}

rule UnsubscribeFromEpic {
    when: UnsubscribeFromEpic(operator, followed)

    -- The person unfollowing: this install's identity, never an argument.
    let person = core/local_host().owner
    let subscription = core/Subscription{subscriber: person, epic: followed}

    requires: subscription != null

    ensures: not exists subscription
    -- A poll claim must not outlive the coverage that made it workable. Every
    -- feed epic in the unfollowed epic's subtree (itself included) that this
    -- person no longer covers once the follow is gone loses any
    -- `core/PollOwner` row held by a host this person owns. Atomic with the
    -- delete above, in the same store write.
    ensures:
        for e in followed.subtree_epics:
            if e.is_feed and not (e in covered_epics_of(person)):
                for claim in core/PollOwners where
                        claim.scope = epic and claim.scope_id = e.id
                            and claim.host.owner = person:
                    not exists claim

    @guidance
        -- THE SUBSCRIBER IS THE CALLER. As for
        -- SubscribeToEpic, the store refuses a subscriber that is not the
        -- identity making the call, so a board cannot unfollow someone else
        -- (releasing their hosts' poll claims). The refusal is the same for a
        -- row that exists and one that does not. The sight check: a caller who
        -- holds no row and cannot see the epic is told "no epic N", not "not
        -- subscribed", so the answer never confirms that a private epic
        -- exists. A caller who still holds a row may always remove it, even
        -- after losing sight of the epic, so a stale follow cannot become
        -- permanent.
        --
        -- RELEASING THE CLAIM. A feed epic this person no longer
        -- follows is invisible to their boards (`list_epics` answers from
        -- `covered_epics` UNION the `own_epics`/`own_tasks` views), so a claim they hold on it
        -- would stop the feed for EVERY board with no error: the owner never
        -- polls it and no other host may. Releasing the row lets the next host
        -- that covers the epic claim it on its next FeedTick (feeds.allium:
        -- FeedTick). If nobody else follows it the feed simply pauses, which
        -- is accepted: no board is asking for its tasks. It resumes when
        -- anyone follows again.
        --
        -- The release is by OWNER, not by this machine: the Subscription is the
        -- person's, so a claim held by their desktop loses coverage exactly as
        -- the laptop's does. A claim held by another person's host is never
        -- touched. An epic that is still covered (a followed ancestor, or a
        -- second followed route) keeps its claim. An epic this identity
        -- created stays visible through the `own_epics` view, so its next tick
        -- re-claims it: that is the narrower thin-subtree case (a) in the
        -- assumption note on `covered_epics`, not a stopped feed.
        --
        -- The epic's tasks stop reaching this board. Reversible at any time by
        -- subscribing again, and carrying nothing with it: a subscription is an
        -- interest, not an assignment, so dropping one changes no task, no
        -- owner and no worktree.
        --
        -- LIVE, on a connected board: the covered_epics view loses the epic
        -- and its rows leave the board as deletes, with no redraw of anything
        -- else and no reconnect.
        --
        -- IT DOES NOT AND CANNOT UNSUBSCRIBE ANYBODY ELSE. The row is looked up
        -- by this install's own identity, so there is no argument through which
        -- a caller could name another subscriber.
        --
        -- The `requires` makes unsubscribing from something unfollowed a
        -- refusal rather than a silent no-op. The asymmetry with SubscribeToEpic
        -- above, which is happily idempotent, is deliberate: subscribing twice
        -- expresses what the caller wanted, and unsubscribing from nothing means
        -- the caller was wrong about the state they were in.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The containment claim of the whole design, stated where it can be read.
--
-- A board asks for its own user board, its subscribed epics, and everything
-- it created itself (own_creations, SubscribeOnceIdentityIsSettled). The
-- interesting half is the converse: nothing else was asked for, so a store
-- that honours the request sends nothing else, and a colleague's private work
-- is not on this machine to be leaked, searched or accidentally rendered.
-- own_creations does not weaken that: it is scoped to rows this identity
-- itself created, so widening it to cover every epic and every unfollowed
-- task never exposes a row belonging to anyone else — the ask stays "mine",
-- just a differently-shaped "mine" than the other two. Nor does covering a
-- followed epic's whole sub-epic tree (BoardConnection.covered_epics): the tree
-- is the epic, and following it is the interest being expressed — a sub-epic
-- is shared work under a shared epic, never somebody's user board.
--
-- Written against the Subscription rows rather than against arriving data
-- because that is the half this system controls. What a store chooses to send
-- an under-subscribed client is the store's to get right; what this board asks
-- for is this file's, and asking for too much is the failure that would be
-- ours.
invariant SubscriptionsCoverOnlyTheOwnBoardAndItsEpics {
    for s in core/Subscriptions:
        s.subscriber = core/local_host().owner implies s.epic != null
}

-- NoWriteIsEverQueued:
-- No write waits anywhere for a store to come back.
--
-- The property behind AWriteWithNoConnectionIsRefused, stated as a fact about
-- the state so that it survives somebody adding a retry to a different layer
-- later. There is no buffer, no outbox, no pending set — not an empty one, none
-- at all. An empty queue is a queue that a well-meaning change can start
-- filling; an absent one has to be designed back in on purpose.
--
-- Prose rather than an expression on purpose. An expression would have to name
-- the collection it claims is empty, and naming it is the first half of adding
-- it. There is nothing here to quantify over, which is exactly the claim.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Actor Declarations
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Connecting, subscribing and reading the indicator are all operator actions
-- (core.allium: Operator). There is deliberately no agent-facing route to any
-- of them: a dispatched agent works inside one task, and changing which epics
-- its operator follows — or taking the board's connection down — is not within
-- that.
--
-- The far side of the connection is core.allium's SharedStore. It is an actor
-- rather than an unnamed outside, because the three events it delivers are a
-- contract this board depends on and a store that delivers only two of them
-- leaves the board in a state with no way out — see
-- StoreAnnouncesEveryTransition on the surface below.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

surface StoreBoundary {
    facing _: core/SharedStore

    context connection: BoardConnection

    exposes:
        connection.server
        connection.subscribed_epics
        -- What the store sends by (SendsOnlyWhatWasSubscribedTo).
        connection.covered_epics

    provides:
        ConnectionAccepted(connection, identity)
            when connection.status = connecting
        ConnectionAttemptFailed(connection, reason)
            when connection.status = connecting
        ConnectionDropped(connection, reason)
            when connection.status = connected
        StoreDeliversRow(connection, row)
            when connection.status = connected
        -- The store's answer to the ask SubscribeOnceIdentityIsSettled sends:
        -- a refusal, or no application within config.connect_timeout. For
        -- FirstConnectionFailureAbortsStartup and
        -- ASubscriptionTheStoreRefusesIsAnOutage.
        InitialSubscriptionRefused(connection, reason)
            when connection.status = connected
        -- The store applied that ask: its rows have arrived. For
        -- FirstConnectionLetsTheBoardDraw.
        InitialSubscriptionApplied(connection)
            when connection.status = connected
        -- An update of a row served through a per-caller view, which reaches
        -- the board as an insert and a delete of the same id, in either
        -- order. For AViewUpdateNeverErasesTheRow.
        ViewRowUpdated(connection, row_old, row_new)
            when connection.status = connected
        -- The rules version the store's module publishes, read once per attempt
        -- for AStoreWithOtherRulesFailsTheAttempt.
        StoreRulesVersionRead(connection, store_rules_version)
            when connection.status = connecting
        StoreRejectedMutation(connection, mutation, reason)
            when connection.status = connected

    @guarantee StoreAnnouncesEveryTransition
        -- Every attempt ends in exactly one of acceptance or failure, and every
        -- accepted connection ends in a drop. A store that lets an attempt hang
        -- without answering, or stops serving without closing, strands the
        -- board in `connecting` or in a `connected` state that is a lie.
        -- config.connect_timeout exists because the first of those cannot be
        -- left to the far end's good behaviour.
        --
        -- THE DROP IS THE TRANSPORT'S TO NOTICE, not the board's to discover.
        -- Nothing above the transport can see a socket die: the board would
        -- find out by failing to do something, and a connected board that is
        -- doing nothing never fails at anything. So a drop is reported, not
        -- polled for — and a transport that cannot report one leaves every rule
        -- below it with no way out of `connected`.
        --
        -- REPORTED ONCE. The same drop announced twice would re-enter
        -- `disconnected` and restart the backoff, turning a long outage into a
        -- hot retry loop, which is what RetryAfterBackoff exists to prevent.

    @guarantee AcceptanceCarriesTheIdentity
        -- Acceptance says who the store believes this client is. The same
        -- client presenting the same credential is the same identity, every
        -- time, across machines and across restarts — that stability is what
        -- host.allium: AdoptUserIdentity relies on, and it is the store's to
        -- provide rather than the board's to reconstruct.

    @guarantee EveryMutationIsAtomicAndAnswered
        -- A mutation sent to the store ends in exactly one of acceptance or
        -- rejection, and either way the store's rows are left in a state that
        -- some single mutation could have produced. Partly applied is not an
        -- outcome. A store that could leave half a mutation standing would make
        -- StoreRejectsAnInvalidMutation's all-or-nothing claim false, and there
        -- is no query a client could run afterwards to find out which half.
        --
        -- A REPLY, NOT ONLY AN EFFECT. Several mutations return something
        -- the caller needs in order to continue — the id of a task it is about
        -- to dispatch, whether a claim was won, what a cleared session was
        -- holding. A store that applied a write and said nothing would leave
        -- those callers guessing, and the guess is a race.
        --
        -- The rejection carries a reason in terms the operator can act on, for
        -- the same reason EveryFailureNamesItself demands one of the indicator.

    @guarantee AnAcceptanceArrivesAfterItsRows
        -- By the time this board hears that a mutation was accepted, every row
        -- that mutation inserted, changed or deleted within this board's
        -- subscriptions has already been delivered. A caller that reads the
        -- board's rows after the answer sees its own write.
        --
        -- This is what the answer is built from rather than a courtesy. A
        -- create learns its new row's id by finding that row among the
        -- delivered ones (own_creations, SubscribeOnceIdentityIsSettled); a
        -- feed cycle learns which tasks the store removed by finding them gone
        -- afterwards (feeds.allium). An answer that could overtake its rows
        -- would make the first report a create as failed when it succeeded,
        -- and the second miss a teardown.
        --
        -- A rejection delivers no rows, because it changed none
        -- (StoreRejectsAnInvalidMutation).

    @guarantee TheStoreEnforcesTheSharedRules
        -- Validation and every derived shared value are the store's, computed
        -- over all the rows rather than over one board's subscription. The
        -- worked example is an epic's status: its children can belong to
        -- different people on different machines, no single board subscribes to
        -- all of them, and two boards each deriving from what they can see
        -- would write two different answers and keep overwriting each other.
        -- See epics.allium: EpicStatusRecalculation.

    @guarantee SendsOnlyWhatWasSubscribedTo
        -- Rows reach this board only for its subscriptions: the epics in
        -- `covered_epics` (the followed ones and their sub-epic trees),
        -- anything the connected identity created or owns (own_creations),
        -- and the other rows the per-caller views serve that identity
        -- (SubscribeOnceIdentityIsSettled: workspaces, memberships,
        -- learnings, links, poll claims, settings, bookmarks, the
        -- super-admin's unseen-item listing), plus the public tables it asks
        -- for directly. Usage is never sent unasked: the board asks for it on
        -- demand.
        -- SubscriptionsCoverOnlyTheOwnBoardAndItsEpics constrains what is
        -- ASKED for; this is the counterpart obligation on what is SENT, and
        -- it is the half a client cannot enforce for itself.

    @guidance
        -- Nothing here names a transport. Sockets, polling and a person
        -- carrying a disk all satisfy this surface; what does not is anything
        -- that cannot report a drop.
}

-- Where a shared write enters this file. The operator's keys, the MCP tools
-- and the board's own workers (the feed runner, the dispatch reactor) each
-- decide WHICH mutation to make (tasks.allium, epics.allium and the rest);
-- every one of them is sent by this board's process, and this file says only
-- where it goes.
surface BoardWrites {
    facing _: core/BoardProcess

    provides:
        SharedRowMutationRequested(mutation)

    @guarantee EverySharedWriteTakesThisPath
        -- A mutation of a shared row reaches the store only as a
        -- SharedRowMutationRequested, so BoardWritesThroughTheStore,
        -- CreatesRequireASettledIdentity and AWriteWithNoConnectionIsRefused
        -- see every one. No caller writes a shared row any other way.
}

surface ConnectionIndicator {
    facing operator: core/Operator

    context connection: BoardConnection

    exposes:
        connection.status
        connection.server
        connection.last_error when connection.status = disconnected | failed
        connection.attempts when connection.status = disconnected

    @guarantee DisconnectedIsAlwaysVisible
        -- A board that is not connected says so, continuously, for as long as
        -- that is true. Not a message that appears once and scrolls away: the
        -- danger is a person trusting a board that stopped updating an hour ago,
        -- and a notification they already dismissed does not protect them.

    @guarantee ConnectedIsQuiet
        -- The healthy state is the overwhelmingly common one, and an indicator
        -- that draws attention to it teaches people to ignore the indicator.
        -- Quiet means no alarm colour and nothing that changes, not nothing
        -- at all: see TheStoreIsAlwaysNamed.

    @guarantee TheStoreIsAlwaysNamed
        -- connection.server is on screen in every status, connected included,
        -- in the same plain style as the rest of the status bar. A board
        -- connected to the wrong store looks exactly like a healthy one — full
        -- of tasks, updating, quiet — and the address is the one thing that
        -- tells them apart at a glance (a board has run on an old store for
        -- two days with nothing on screen to say so). The operator
        -- chose this over showing it only for a named store.

    @guarantee EveryFailureNamesItself
        -- `disconnected` and `failed` both carry a reason an operator can act
        -- on — which host, refused or timed out, which two identities. "Sync
        -- error" is not an acceptable message for the only thing on screen
        -- telling somebody why their colleague's work is missing.

    @guidance
        -- `attempts` is exposed while disconnected because it is the difference
        -- between "just now" and "for the last twenty minutes", and those call
        -- for different reactions from the person reading it. It is not exposed
        -- in `failed`: retrying is not happening and a count would imply it is.
}

surface EpicSubscriptionToggle {
    facing operator: core/Operator

    context epic: core/Epic

    let connection = the_board_connection()

    exposes:
        epic in connection.subscribed_epics

    provides:
        SubscribeToEpic(operator, epic)
            when not (epic in connection.subscribed_epics)
        UnsubscribeFromEpic(operator, epic)
            when epic in connection.subscribed_epics

    @guarantee SubscriptionStateIsVisibleBeforeItIsChanged
        -- Whether this board follows an epic is readable from the epic itself.
        -- A toggle whose current position is invisible is a button that does
        -- one of two opposite things.

    @guidance
        -- Deliberately attached to the epic rather than gathered into a
        -- subscription manager somewhere. The moment a person decides they care
        -- about an epic is the moment they are looking at it, and a separate
        -- screen is a screen they have to know exists.
        --
        -- The two actions are guarded into a toggle rather than offered as a
        -- pair, so that the operation available is always the one that changes
        -- something.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "What is a board supposed to do with a change whose fate is unknown? EveryMutationIsAtomicAndAnswered admits acceptance and rejection, and a transport has a third outcome: the change was SENT and then the connection went, or the store never answered within config.mutation_timeout. It may or may not have been applied, and nothing here says who is told what. Re-sending is the one answer that is definitely wrong — it is the queue NoWriteIsEverQueued refuses, with the added hazard that the original may have landed."

open question "Does a store that keeps accepting the connection and refusing its initial subscription ever get backed off? ConnectionAccepted resets `attempts` to 0 on acceptance, and ASubscriptionTheStoreRefusesIsAnOutage then adds one, so every cycle of that failure waits config.reconnect_backoff_base and the wait never grows towards config.reconnect_backoff_max — a refusal that lasts an hour is retried roughly every second for an hour, which is the hot loop the ceiling's own comment says the backoff exists to prevent. Should `attempts` reset only once the initial subscription is applied, rather than on acceptance?"

open question "Should the board expose a way to retry NOW, rather than waiting out the backoff? An operator who has just fixed the store knows something the backoff does not. Left out for the moment because config.reconnect_backoff_max is a minute, which is short enough that the button might only exist to be pressed impatiently."

open question "Is a periodic refresh still worth keeping now that rows arrive unasked? It is a backstop against a delivery that never comes — a callback the SDK drops, a subscription that silently stops — and it costs nothing when nothing has changed. But a backstop nobody can observe failing is also a backstop nobody knows has failed, and the board would be quietly polling its way through a broken subscription rather than reporting one."

open question "Is `failed` recoverable without restarting the board? It is terminal in the graph, so today an identity conflict is resolved by fixing the credential and starting again. Whether a resolved conflict should be able to re-open the connection in place is undecided, and depends on a remedy flow nobody has designed."
